      <h2>What goes in each issue</h2>

      <h3>1. Tip</h3>
      <p>One specific, useful observation drawn from a recent audit, build, or post. Two to four sentences. Plain language. Always teach something concrete the reader can use without me &mdash; whether or not they ever hire me.</p>

      <h3>2. Ship</h3>
      <p>One thing I shipped recently &mdash; a new blog post, a new sample site, an updated service page, a public changelog entry. One or two sentences plus a link. Demonstrates the agency is active and improving.</p>

      <h3>3. Ask</h3>
      <p>One soft CTA. Not the same one every time. Sometimes "got a friend in [trade] still on Wix? Forward this." Sometimes "if your own site is on the list of things-to-fix, the free audit is open." Never a hard pitch.</p>

      <h2>Why bi-weekly</h2>
      <p>Weekly is too much for me to write at the standard I want; monthly is too thin to stay top-of-mind. Bi-weekly is the cadence that produces real signal without filler. If I do not have something genuinely useful that week, I skip it.</p>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>The list runs on <a href="https://buttondown.com" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">Buttondown</a> &mdash; an indie, privacy-first email platform. Your email is used to send the newsletter, nothing else. No tracking pixels, no third-party retargeting. Unsubscribe in one click; the link is in every issue. The full <a href="/privacy-policy/" class="inline-link">privacy policy</a> covers the rest.</p>

      <h2>Archive</h2>
      <p>Past issues will appear here once issue 3 is live. Until then, the recent <a href="/blog/" class="inline-link">blog posts</a> cover the same territory in long form.</p>
